  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/e/ed/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_95.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Odin summons Thor home to provide rain for Asgard, which has been hit by a drought.&nbsp; Thor starts the rain, which he says will last a week, and then he'll return to stop it.&nbsp; Thor then returns to Earth to help Professor Zaxton \"demonstrate the strange android which my other identity, Dr. Don Blake, invented!\"\n\nSure enough, the android comes out and immediately solves math problems and then shows that it can withstand a blow from Thor's hammer.&nbsp; Zaxton states that Dr. Blake gave the android unbreakable skin - some kind of plastic tissue.&nbsp; Zaxton goes on to say that an army of these ultra-intelligent androids would be invincible!&nbsp; Suddenly, the control circuit shorts out, and the android is about to explode, but Thor wraps it with metal bands and slides his hammer into them and hurls the android out the window where it explodes.\n\nThor seems put off, suspecting that Zaxton might have done something on purpose.&nbsp; Back in his office as Don Blake, he finds Professor Zaxton waiting for him to demonstrate a \"duplicating machine.\"&nbsp; He thinks Blake's android was a mechanical miracle, so he wants Blake's help duplicating human beings!&nbsp; Blake is horrified, but Zaxton is holding Jane Foster prisoner!&nbsp; Turns out he <i>did</i> sabotage the android because he was jealous.\n\nBlake could turn to Thor, but he decides to bide his time, and <i>whoa</i>, after only a \"long, long night\" in the lab, they're ready to test his machine, which he starts with an alley cat, producing many duplicates.&nbsp; He tells Blake where Jane is, so he turns to Thor, but Zaxton sees him (and now knows Blake is Thor) and zaps him with the ray.&nbsp; Now there are two Thors!&nbsp; The physical structure is the same, but the personalities are opposite, so the duplicate Thor protects Zaxton's machine from being smashed by Thor, and to give the duplicate an advantage, Zaxton duplicates a second hammer for Thor 2!&nbsp; Zaxton of course wants \"total, absolute power!\"&nbsp; He plans to use duplicate Thor to make him \"ruler of the world!\"\n\nAs Thor attempts to escape, Zaxton literally uses his device to create a duplicate of an entire office building for him to bump into!&nbsp; Later, he creates \"dozens of airliners\" to block his path!&nbsp; Thor 2 catches up to him and hurls both hammers, which strike Thor in the chest, but he feels nothing and then notices the inscription on the hammer, noting that the bearer will only have the power of Thor if he is worthy - the evil Thor is not, so he cannot harm Thor at all, and one blow from the real uru hammer is enough to send him fading away \"into the nothingness whence he came!\"\n\nTurning back to Zaxton, the scientist desperately creates a duplicate of himself so Thor can't tell which is which, but loses his grip on the machine.&nbsp; Desperately trying to grab it, he instead falls to his death, the machine shattering as well.&nbsp; The duplicate professor has his opposite personality \"without his streak of villainy.\"&nbsp; So Thor is happy that the duplicate will live to \"do good where the original did bad!\"&nbsp; (And he doesn't seem at all concerned now that the duplicate Zaxton would also have the knowledge that Don Blake is Thor, presumably because he is \"good\" I guess.)\n\nHe goes to rescue Jane, then days later returns to Asgard to stop the rain as promised.&nbsp; Odin is happy he remembered to come back, but Thor assures him that \"no matter what adventures may summon me to the world below, <i>this</i> is the land my heart calls home!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Another sour taste in my mouth after reading this.&nbsp; Blake's invention of the android comes out of <i>nowhere</i> and seems to serve no purpose at all except to introduce Professor Zaxton.&nbsp; (Will we ever see this android invention again?&nbsp; I doubt it.)&nbsp; The duplicating machine is just a completely silly device, and the fact that one long night of the two of them conferring together was enough to perfect it so that it could work on living creatures?&nbsp; <i>Come on</i>!&nbsp; Plus, why on Earth would Zaxton have created it with the notion of imbedding the duplicates with the \"opposite personalities?\"&nbsp; Wouldn't Blake have seen this obvious problem while they were working together?&nbsp; Did Zaxton really suddenly go from jealous scientist to kidnapper, then to a madman who was bent on being the ruler of the world?&nbsp; (What's <i>with</i> these people?)\n\nAnd what about the duplicate office building and \"dozens\" of airplanes, complete with duplicate copies of hundreds, if not <i>thousands</i> of people?&nbsp; What chaos this must have caused.&nbsp; We know the duplicate professor will apparently be able to live on, as Thor indicates on the last page, so won't these other duplicates also live on, sharing the same past, memories, and social security numbers as their originals, but with opposite personalities?&nbsp; Talk about identity theft!&nbsp; <i>Ugh</i>, what an absolute mess of a story, and quite frankly an inane and silly plot, filled with gaping holes - totally deserving of the poor rating!\n\nThe book is not <i>completely</i> without any positive aspects however.&nbsp; I <i>do</i> like the fact that over the last handful of issues, we're slowly seeing Thor accepting that his true heritage is on Asgard.&nbsp; At the beginning, it seemed like Thor viewed Odin as a curiosity, and now we see him happy to be in Asgard and to call Odin \"father\" without any reservations.&nbsp; We see more and more signs that the two identities of Thor and Blake and beginning to blend together, probably never again to read comments to the effect of \"Oh, it's Odin, the legendary father of Thor!\"&nbsp; No, it's becoming \"my father\" - to the betterment of the magazine.&nbsp; This character will continue to have an aspect to him that no other Marvel character to date will ever be able to...<i>uh</i>...duplicate (<i>argh</i>, sorry for the pun)&nbsp; This saves the issue from an ever lower rating in the end...\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nAnd oh my, once again, Joe Sinnott does pencils, and all I have to say about the handle on that hammer is that Thor needn't worry about getting a cane when he finally gets old, because he can just use his hammer - that thing has to be at least 3 feet long!&nbsp; <i>Good grief!</i>",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}